Logical Files (DDS) - Sort, Select and Omit examples
======================================================

On IBM i (AS/400), a Logical File (LF) does not store data itself.
It gives you a different "view" over one or more Physical Files (PF):
a different sort order (keys), and/or a subset of the records
(SELECT / OMIT), without copying or duplicating the data.

All DDS source lines below follow the classic fixed-column layout:

	• Column 6  = Form type, always "A" for DDS
	• Column 7  = "*" marks a full comment line
	• Column 17 = "R" starts a record format, "K" defines a key field,
	              "S" is a SELECT condition, "O" is an OMIT condition
	• Column 19+ = Field name / keyword / values

Order rules DDS actually enforces (the #1 source of "why won't this
compile"):

	1. File-level keywords (UNIQUE, DYNSLT, a file-wide ALTSEQ, etc.)
	   come first, on their own line with no name/type, BEFORE the
	   "R" record format line.
	2. The "R" line starts the record format, followed by its field
	   entries and its "K" key entries - these two can be mixed in
	   any order relative to each other.
	3. Every "K" entry for a record format must appear before any
	   "S"/"O" entry for that same record format - select/omit is
	   always the LAST group. A "K" placed after an "S"/"O" line
	   breaks this order.
	4. A field used in "S"/"O" must already be part of the record
	   format's field list - either given its own field-level entry,
	   or already used in a "K" entry. Referenced only on the S/O
	   line and nowhere else - and DDS raises CPD7948, "select/omit
	   field name not found in logical file" - unless the file also
	   specifies DYNSLT, which relaxes that requirement.
	5. A file-level keyword coded in the wrong spot (after "R", or
	   nested under a field/key/select-omit entry) raises CPD7486,
	   "keyword specified at incorrect level."
	6. Numeric fields (P/S/B) always need BOTH digits and decimal
	   positions if you restate their attributes in the LF - "10P 0",
	   never just "10P".


Underlying Physical File (for reference)
-----------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC
     A            CUSNO          6P 0
     A            CUSNAME       30A
     A            STATE          2A
     A            STATUS         1A
     A          K CUSNO

Meaning:
	• CUSTOMER (PF) has one record format, CUSTREC
	• CUSNO   = customer number (packed 6,0) - arrival key
	• CUSNAME = customer name
	• STATE   = 2-char state code
	• STATUS  = 'A' active, 'I' inactive, 'D' deleted


Example 1 - Simple keyed logical file (sort only)
----------------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K STATE
     A          K CUSNAME

Meaning:
	• PFILE(CUSTOMER) - this LF is built over the CUSTOMER physical file
	• No SELECT/OMIT is used, so every record from CUSTOMER is visible
	• K STATE, then K CUSNAME - records are presented in ascending
	  order by STATE, then by CUSNAME within each STATE (a compound key)
	• This is the DDS equivalent of "ORDER BY STATE, CUSNAME"


Example 2 - Descending key
------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K CUSNO                    DESCEND

Meaning:
	• DESCEND reverses the sort order for that key field only
	• Highest CUSNO is read first - useful for "most recent record" views


Example 3 - SELECT (only include matching records)
-------------------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K STATE
     A          K CUSNAME
     A          S STATE                    COMP(EQ 'CA')

Meaning:
	• Both K entries come first, then the "S" entry last - S/O must
	  always follow every K entry in the same record format
	• Naming the field directly on the S line ("S STATE") is clearer
	  than leaving it blank and relying on "the field named just
	  above," especially once keys are grouped ahead of it
	• COMP(EQ 'CA') - only rows where STATE = 'CA' are visible
	  through this logical file
	• A logical file can carry several S entries; when several
	  conditions apply to the *same* field they are OR'ed together,
	  conditions on *different* fields are AND'ed together


Example 4 - SELECT with a list of VALUES
--------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K STATE
     A          K CUSNAME
     A          S STATE                    VALUES('CA' 'NV' 'OR')

Meaning:
	• Same ordering rule as Example 3 - both keys before the S entry
	• VALUES(...) selects any record whose STATE matches one of the
	  listed values - equivalent to SQL "WHERE STATE IN ('CA','NV','OR')"


Example 5 - OMIT (exclude matching records)
------------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K CUSNAME
     A          O STATUS                   COMP(EQ 'D')

Meaning:
	• The "O" entry works like "S" but excludes the matching rows instead
	• COMP(EQ 'D') - records flagged STATUS = 'D' (deleted) never appear
	  through this logical file, even though they still exist in the PF
	• SELECT and OMIT can be combined: e.g. SELECT active customers in a
	  RANGE of states, then OMIT a specific customer number


Example 6 - SELECT with a RANGE, plus OMIT, combined
----------------------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K STATE
     A          K CUSNAME
     A          S CUSNO                    RANGE(100000 199999)
     A          O STATUS                   COMP(EQ 'I')

Meaning:
	• Both keys (STATE, CUSNAME) are grouped first, then the S and O
	  entries follow - S/O is always the last group, listed in the
	  order the conditions should be AND'ed together
	• RANGE(low high) selects only records whose CUSNO falls between
	  100000 and 199999 inclusive
	• The O entry then OMITs any of those that are Inactive ('I')
	• Result: active customers with CUSNO 100000-199999, sorted by
	  STATE then CUSNAME


Example 7 - Unique key and dynamic select/omit
---------------------------------------------------

     A                                      UNIQUE
     A                                      DYNSLT
     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K CUSNO
     A          S STATUS                   COMP(EQ 'A')

Meaning:
	• UNIQUE and DYNSLT are both FILE-level keywords - each goes on
	  its own line, with no name/type, BEFORE the "R" record format
	  line. Coding one of them after a field, key, or select/omit
	  entry instead - e.g. tacking DYNSLT onto the end of an "S"
	  entry - raises CPD7486, "keyword specified at incorrect level"
	• UNIQUE - duplicate-key protection, so a key value can appear
	  only once through the logical file
	• DYNSLT - selection is evaluated dynamically at OPEN/READ time
	  instead of being resolved into an access path at build time;
	  handy when the select/omit values change often or depend on
	  a value passed in from the program (avoids rebuilding the LF)
	• Trade-off: DYNSLT means NO persistent keyed access path gets
	  built for this file at all - so a K field on a DYNSLT file is
	  NOT honored as a sort order by anything (not RUNQRY, not native
	  RPG reads, not SQL). Only reach for DYNSLT when the select/omit
	  VALUE genuinely changes at runtime (e.g. passed in as a program
	  variable); if it's a fixed constant like COMP(EQ 'NY'), leave
	  DYNSLT off so the key actually gets built as a real access path


Example 8 - Renamed/derived fields, alternate collating, duplicate order
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A            CUSTNBR                    RENAME(CUSNO)
     A            FULLNAME      36           CONCAT(CUSNAME STATE)
     A            CUSNAME                    TEXT('Customer full name')
     A                                        COLHDG('CUST' 'NAME')
     A          K STATE
     A                                        ALTSEQ(MYXLATE)
     A          K CUSTNBR
     A                                        FIFO

Meaning:
	• RENAME(CUSNO) - CUSTNBR is what this logical view calls the field,
	  even though it's physically stored as CUSNO in the PF; lets a
	  program use a friendlier/standard name without touching the data
	• CONCAT(CUSNAME STATE) - FULLNAME doesn't exist in the physical
	  file at all; it's built on the fly by joining CUSNAME and STATE
	  together into one 36-character field
	• TEXT / COLHDG - documentation-only keywords; TEXT is the field's
	  description, COLHDG supplies column-heading text for displays and
	  query tools - neither one changes the data
	• ALTSEQ(MYXLATE) - sorts STATE using an alternate collating
	  sequence table instead of normal EBCDIC order (e.g. for
	  case-insensitive or language-specific sorting); ABSVAL is a
	  sibling keyword that sorts a signed numeric key by its absolute
	  value instead
	• FIFO - when two records share the same CUSTNBR key value, the
	  oldest-arrival one is returned first; LIFO reverses that, and
	  FCFO (first-changed-first-out) is a third option based on update
	  order rather than arrival order
	• Not shown here, but related: FORMAT(fmtname) copies an entire
	  record format's field list from another file instead of retyping
	  every field, and REFFLD pulls one field's attributes from a field
	  reference file - both save typing when several files share fields

A logical file doesn't need a key at all - SELECT/OMIT alone is legal:

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A            STATUS
     A          S                          COMP(EQ 'A')

Meaning:
	• No "K" entry - records come back in the physical file's arrival
	  sequence, just filtered to STATUS = 'A'


Example 9 - Multi-format logical file and Join logical file
------------------------------------------------------------------

A single logical file can bundle more than one record format, each
pointed at a different physical file - still not a join, just two
independent "windows" sharing one file object:

     A          R CUSTFMT                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A            CUSNO
     A            CUSNAME
     A
     A          R ORDFMT                   PFILE(ORDERS)
     A            ORDNO
     A            CUSNO
     A            ORDDATE

Meaning:
	• CUSTFMT reads/writes CUSTOMER, ORDFMT reads/writes ORDERS
	• A program opens this one LF and can READ either format by name
	• Useful for menu-driver style programs that want several files
	  under one file object; the two formats never combine rows

A Join Logical File (JLF) is different - it actually combines rows
from two or more physical files into one record format, similar to a
SQL join. It is read-only: no WRITE/UPDATE/DELETE through a JLF.

     A          R JOINREC                  JFILE(CUSTOMER ORDERS)
     A          J                          JOIN(1 2)
     A            CUSNO                     JFLD(CUSNO)
     A            CUSNAME
     A            ORDNO
     A            ORDDATE
     A                                      JDFTVAL

Meaning:
	• JFILE(CUSTOMER ORDERS) - the physical files being joined;
	  CUSTOMER is file 1 (primary), ORDERS is file 2 (secondary)
	• The "J" entry with JOIN(1 2) starts the join condition between
	  those two files
	• JFLD(CUSNO) - the field the two files are matched on (it must
	  exist in both physical files); this is the join's "ON" clause
	• JDFTVAL - if a CUSTOMER has no matching ORDERS row, still return
	  the customer with default/blank values for the order fields;
	  this is what makes it behave like a LEFT OUTER JOIN instead of
	  the plain INNER JOIN a JLF does by default
	• Real join DDS needs a few more entries (JDUPSEQ to control which
	  match wins when several secondary rows qualify, etc.) - this is
	  a simplified illustration of the core idea, not compile-ready


Example 10 - Multiple logical files over the same physical file
------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing stops you from building several logical files against the
same PFILE - this is the normal, everyday reason LFs exist: one set
of physical rows, several different "views" over it, no duplicated
data. Only the file name has to differ; what actually varies between
them is the key, the select/omit, and/or the field list.

Sorted by customer name, every column, no filter:

     A          R CUSTREC                  PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K CUSNAME

Filtered to one state only, sorted by customer number:

     A          R CUSTREC2                 PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A          K CUSNO
     A          S STATE                    COMP(EQ 'CA')

A trimmed-down "lookup" view - just two fields, arrival order, active only:

     A          R CUSTLKUP                 PFILE(CUSTOMER)
     A            CUSNO
     A            CUSNAME
     A          O STATUS                   COMP(NE 'A')

Meaning:
	• Each one is its own object (CUSTL1/CUSTL2/CUSTL3-style names in
	  real life) - that's the only thing that MUST differ
	• Record format names were also changed here (CUSTREC, CUSTREC2,
	  CUSTLKUP) - not strictly required, but if a program ever opens
	  two of these logical files at the same time, RPG/COBOL won't
	  allow two open files sharing one record format name unless you
	  add PREFIX on the F-spec, so distinct names avoid that headache
	• The third LF lists only CUSNO/CUSNAME, so that's all a program
	  reading through it will ever see, even though CUSTOMER has more
	  fields - handy for exposing a "safe" subset of a table
	• If two LFs end up with identical keys AND identical select/omit,
	  the system automatically shares one access path between them
	  instead of building/maintaining two identical indexes - keeping
	  key/select-omit consistent across a family of LFs over one PF
	  isn't just neat, it saves real DASD and CPU


Common compile errors (and what they actually mean)
----------------------------------------------------------
	• CPD7948 - "Key, select, or omit field name not found in
	  logical file." The field on your S/O line was never declared
	  as a plain field or a key elsewhere in the record format. Add
	  a field-level entry for it, or add DYNSLT to the file.
	• CPD7486 - "Keyword specified at incorrect level." A file-level
	  keyword (DYNSLT, UNIQUE, a file-wide ALTSEQ, etc.) was coded
	  after the "R" line, or nested under a field/key/select-omit
	  entry, instead of on its own line before "R".
	• Decimal-positions error on a numeric field - a P/S/B field was
	  restated in the LF with digits but no decimal positions (e.g.
	  "10P" instead of "10P 0"). Either give both, or don't restate
	  the field's attributes at all and let it inherit from the PF.
	• A "K" appearing after an "S"/"O" entry for the same record
	  format breaks the required order (all keys, then all
	  select/omit) even when no single line looks wrong on its own -
	  move every K entry above every S/O entry for that format.


Runtime gotchas (compiles fine, but doesn't do what you expect)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
	• "My K DESCEND isn't honored in SQL." SQL never guarantees row
	  order unless you say so - a table/view/logical file is just an
	  unordered set to the relational engine. "SELECT * FROM ..." with
	  no ORDER BY can come back in arrival order, index order, or
	  whatever the optimizer picked that run - never rely on it. Add
	  "ORDER BY CLMNO DESC" explicitly; if it matches the LF's key,
	  the optimizer will often reuse that access path to satisfy it
	  cheaply, so the key still isn't wasted effort.
	• "My K DESCEND isn't honored even in RUNQRY / native reads
	  either." Check for DYNSLT on the file. DYNSLT skips building a
	  real access path in favor of dynamically testing select/omit at
	  read time - as a side effect, the K field's sort order is never
	  materialized either, so NOTHING sorts by it, native or SQL. Fix:
	  only use DYNSLT when the select/omit value truly varies at
	  runtime; drop it for fixed/constant conditions so the key builds
	  a real, honored access path.


Quick reference
-------------------
	• K        -> defines a key field (sort sequence)
	• DESCEND  -> sorts that key field high to low
	• S        -> SELECT: keep only records matching the condition
	• O        -> OMIT: drop records matching the condition
	• COMP     -> compares a field to a single value (EQ, NE, GT, LT, GE, LE)
	• VALUES   -> compares a field to a list of values (OR'ed)
	• RANGE    -> compares a field to a low/high pair (inclusive)
	• UNIQUE   -> enforces unique key values (file-level - before the R line)
	• DYNSLT   -> evaluates SELECT/OMIT at runtime (file-level - before the R line)
	• PFILE    -> names the physical file(s) this logical file is based on
	• RENAME   -> exposes a physical field under a different name
	• CONCAT   -> builds a field by joining two or more physical fields
	• FORMAT   -> reuses another file's entire record format definition
	• REFFLD   -> pulls one field's attributes from a field reference file
	• TEXT / COLHDG -> documentation only: description / column headings
	• ALTSEQ   -> sorts a key using an alternate collating sequence
	• ABSVAL   -> sorts a signed numeric key by absolute value
	• FIFO/LIFO/FCFO -> order for records that share the same key value
	• JFILE/JOIN/JFLD/JDFTVAL -> build a read-only Join Logical File
